// Expanded chapter notes based on the supplied Research Methodology course transcript.
// Each chapter is written as study paragraphs for exam revision, not as a verbatim transcript.
const chapterStudyNotes = [
[
 {title:'1. Research as a different kind of learning',points:[
  'Course-based education is usually designed around a known body of knowledge. A syllabus sets out topics, a teacher presents established material, and assignments or examinations check whether students have learned it. Research begins in a different place: the answer is not fully known in advance. The researcher first learns what is established, then investigates a question for which existing explanations, evidence or methods are incomplete. The course discussion uses this difference to explain why research degrees cannot be understood simply as “more coursework.” They require a change in the learner’s role, from receiving knowledge to taking responsibility for creating and evaluating it.',
  'An undergraduate or taught postgraduate course can still involve discovery, projects and independent thought. The distinction is one of emphasis and expectation. In a research degree, a thesis is expected to contain work that is specific to the student and contributes something original. The contribution can be modest and carefully bounded; it need not revolutionize an entire discipline. It must be distinguishable from prior work, based on a defensible method, supported by evidence and explained in relation to the field. Reproducing another person’s answer or repeating a known result without a meaningful purpose does not meet that central expectation.',
  'Research therefore combines learning with discovery. Learning is not a preliminary stage that ends once the researcher knows enough. It continues while questions are refined, methods are selected, evidence is collected and interpretations are challenged. A researcher studies the field to understand its concepts and methods, but also searches for places where an assumption can be examined, a gap can be addressed or an existing approach can be improved. The work is both intellectual and practical: ideas must be made precise enough to investigate and the investigation must be carried out carefully enough to support conclusions.'
 ]},
 {title:'2. The inquiry cycle and the role of evidence',points:[
  'A useful way to remember the broad research cycle is: understand the background, frame a question, choose an approach, gather evidence, analyze it, interpret what it means, and communicate the contribution. This is a guide, not a rigid recipe. Research often loops back. An early analysis may reveal that a question is too broad; an experiment may show that a measurement needs improvement; a literature search may expose a more important question than the one first proposed. Revision is part of responsible inquiry, not a sign that the project has failed.',
  'The question should guide the method. If the aim is to describe a phenomenon, a descriptive approach may be appropriate. If the aim is to estimate a quantity, the researcher needs observations and an estimation method. If the aim is to compare conditions, the design must make a meaningful comparison possible. The specific techniques differ by discipline, but the reasoning is shared: explain why the evidence collected can answer the question, what assumptions are being made, and what conclusions the evidence permits. A polished method that does not address the research question is not a good research design.',
  'Evidence is not the same as a conclusion. Measurements, documents, observations, interviews or calculations become evidence through a transparent process of selection, analysis and interpretation. A conclusion should follow from that evidence and should be limited to the studied conditions. Researchers should separate what they observed from what they infer, and identify uncertainty or limitations that matter. Unexpected results deserve examination and recording. They may reflect an error, an unrecognized condition or a genuine feature of the system; deciding which requires investigation rather than simply discarding the result.'
 ]},
 {title:'3. Novelty, context and the research gap',points:[
  'A claim of originality depends on knowing what already exists. The course emphasizes that novelty can take more than one form. A method that is familiar in one field may be useful when applied to a different problem; a new technique may provide value in a well-studied area. The label “new” is not enough. The researcher must establish what is new relative to the relevant literature and explain why that difference matters. A literature survey is therefore a core part of the logic of research, not merely a background chapter written to satisfy a format.',
  'A research gap is not simply a topic that has few papers. It is a defensible opening in knowledge or practice. A gap might concern an unanswered question, conflicting results, a method that has not been tested under relevant conditions, a limitation in current explanations, or a need to extend an established result. The researcher should show how the gap follows from the literature, why it deserves attention, and what kind of contribution could address it. A good answer in an examination distinguishes “little has been written” from a well-supported reason for further study.',
  'Research is also placed in a current context. A useful study connects to what other researchers are doing and makes clear whether it extends, tests, clarifies or challenges existing knowledge. Context helps readers judge value and prevents a project from appearing disconnected from the field. It also helps researchers avoid accidental duplication. Even a technically successful experiment may add little if it repeats an established finding without explaining a reason to repeat it. Conversely, replication can be valuable when the purpose is to check reliability, investigate a disputed result or test whether an effect holds in a new context.'
 ]},
 {title:'4. Uncertainty, failure and persistence',points:[
  'The course panel describes the beginning of research as hazy because the frontier of knowledge is not organized like a finished textbook. A textbook presents material after it has been selected, tested and arranged for teaching. A research paper records a much more specific investigation, and its importance may not be obvious at first. Researchers must work while some elements remain uncertain: the problem may change, a method may fail, an experiment may not produce the expected pattern, and the eventual use of a result may be difficult to predict.',
  'Failure in an attempt is information if it is documented and examined. An unsuccessful experiment can identify a faulty assumption, a limitation in equipment, an uncontrolled variable or an unsuitable method. The researcher should ask what the failure reveals, what should be changed, and whether the result rules out an explanation. Simply repeating the same failed procedure without learning from it is not perseverance. Nor is changing procedures without keeping a record. Persistence means continuing thoughtfully, recognizing incremental progress and making reasoned adjustments in response to evidence.',
  'A researcher should not mistake uncertainty for lack of structure. Good records, regular meetings, milestones and clear decisions provide structure without pretending that every result can be predicted. Time management matters because research is open-ended and can expand indefinitely. Researchers need to decide what evidence is sufficient for the stated question and when the contribution is adequately supported. This balance—remaining open to discovery while keeping a focused question and realistic plan—is a practical skill developed over the course of a research degree.'
 ]},
 {title:'5. The researcher, advisor and research community',points:[
  'The course discussion places importance on attitude, interest and a willingness to learn. A student does not have to begin as an expert in the exact topic; knowledge can be acquired through study and work. However, the student must be prepared to engage, take initiative and accept responsibility for progress. Research scholars also need to communicate their ideas, discuss uncertainty and seek feedback. Regular conversations with an advisor can help the student clarify thinking even when the advisor does not immediately provide a solution.',
  'Advisor relationships vary. Some advisors help define a problem early; others expect the student to explore literature and propose a problem. Some are closely involved in day-to-day decisions, while others provide periodic guidance. Students should learn how a potential advisor works, what kinds of projects are available and what communication patterns are expected. The course notes that this relationship is a factor in choosing an advisor. No single supervision style is universally right for every student, so expectations and responsibilities should be discussed rather than assumed.',
  'The student’s role changes with experience. Early in a project, an advisor may help interpret results because they have broader experience of the field. Over time, the student is expected to become the expert in the thesis topic and to make increasingly independent judgments. This is not a reason to stop seeking guidance. It is evidence that the student can frame problems, assess evidence and explain the next useful direction. Peers and collaborators also matter: discussion can expose assumptions, bring different skills and help researchers work through difficult stages.'
 ]},
 {title:'6. Exam approach: explain, compare and apply',points:[
  'For an exam question asking “What is research?”, avoid giving only a dictionary definition. Explain the movement from existing knowledge to a question and then to a supported contribution. Make clear that inquiry involves learning, discovery, method, evidence, interpretation and communication. If asked how research differs from a course degree, contrast the expected outputs and the level of independence rather than arguing that one requires more work than the other.',
  'For a question about originality, explain why the literature must be surveyed, identify a plausible kind of gap and show how the proposed work would address it. For a question about failure, distinguish a failed attempt from an uninformative process: discuss documentation, diagnosis and revision. For a question about supervision, describe the advisor’s possible role in perspective and feedback while recognizing the student’s growing responsibility.',
  'When applying these ideas to a scenario, state the research question first. Then identify what is already known, what remains uncertain, what evidence would help, and how the method would connect to the question. Finish by noting assumptions or limits. This structure makes an answer specific and reasoned instead of listing general qualities such as “hard work” or “innovation” without showing how they affect the research.'
 ]}
],
[
 {title:'1. The role of data analysis',points:[
  'Data analysis is the process of examining collected observations to answer a research question. The course places analysis within a larger path: a process or phenomenon produces data through measurement, and analysis helps convert those data into descriptions, estimates, comparisons or explanations. The process being studied need not be an industrial machine. It may be biological, social or engineering-related. Likewise, a sensor need not be a physical instrument; in a survey, the respondent and the procedure for recording responses form part of the measurement system.',
  'The purpose of analysis determines the tools and interpretation. A researcher may want to describe a dataset, estimate unknown quantities, develop a model, extract informative features, classify observations, test a hypothesis or detect faults. These goals are related but not identical. For example, a descriptive summary characterizes observed data, while a prediction estimates an outcome for a new case. A classification assigns observations to categories, while fault detection compares observations with expected behavior. Before choosing a technique, state what the analysis is meant to accomplish.',
  'Analysis does not repair a poorly designed study automatically. The way data were sampled, measured and recorded affects the conclusions that can be drawn. Ask whether the observations represent the question, whether units and definitions are consistent, and whether missing or unusual values have been handled transparently. Keep raw data unchanged and document transformations. A clear path from raw observations to reported results lets another researcher understand what was done and assess whether the interpretation is justified.'
 ]},
 {title:'2. Describe data before making claims',points:[
  'Begin by inspecting the data. Check the number of observations, variable names, units, ranges, missing values and obvious recording errors. Summary statistics such as mean, median, variance or standard deviation can describe aspects of a distribution, but no single statistic tells the whole story. A mean can be strongly affected by extreme values; a spread measure does not explain why the values vary. Pair numerical summaries with visual inspection when appropriate.',
  'Visualization is an important part of analysis, not merely decoration. A plot can reveal trends, clusters, outliers, nonlinear patterns or changes over time that a table of values might hide. Choose a plot appropriate to the variables and purpose. Label axes, identify units, use scales honestly and avoid visual choices that exaggerate a difference. A chart should make the evidence easier to inspect, not persuade the reader through styling.',
  'Unusual observations deserve investigation rather than automatic deletion. Verify whether a value is a transcription or instrument error, a valid extreme case, or evidence that the assumed model does not describe all observations. If a value is excluded or transformed, explain the reason and assess whether that decision changes the conclusions. Transparent treatment of data quality is essential because selective cleaning can create an appearance of support that the full dataset does not provide.'
 ]},
 {title:'3. Estimation and hypothesis testing',points:[
  'Parameter estimation uses observed data to infer an unknown quantity. In the course discussion, “parameter” is used broadly: it may refer to a parameter in a model or a statistical property such as an average, variance or median. An estimate is not the unknown truth itself. It is an inference based on a particular sample, measurement process and set of assumptions. Good reporting identifies the quantity estimated and provides an indication of uncertainty when the method allows it.',
  'Hypothesis testing evaluates a claim using data. A researcher states a null or reference claim and an alternative claim, chooses a test suited to the data and design, and compares the observed evidence with what would be expected under the reference assumption. A p-value is interpreted relative to that assumption and the test procedure; it is not the probability that the hypothesis is true. Statistical significance does not automatically imply practical importance, and lack of significance does not prove that no effect exists.',
  'An exam answer should connect the test to the research question and identify assumptions. Consider the type of outcome, how observations were collected, independence or dependence among observations, distributional requirements and sample size. If assumptions do not fit, the result may be misleading. Report the estimate and uncertainty as well as a test outcome where relevant. Avoid treating a single threshold as a substitute for understanding the magnitude, direction and context of an effect.'
 ]},
 {title:'4. Models, prediction and feature analysis',points:[
  'A model represents a relationship or process in a simplified form. Researchers may develop models to understand how variables relate or to predict a quantity of interest. The model’s usefulness depends on the purpose: a model that predicts adequately in a limited range may not explain the underlying mechanism, and a mechanistic model may require assumptions that limit where it can be applied. State what the model represents and what it is intended to do.',
  'Model development involves choosing variables, specifying a relationship, estimating its unknown parameters and comparing its behavior with observations. A model should be assessed on evidence that is not merely the data used to fit it when prediction is the goal. Consider residual patterns, error, fit, stability and performance under relevant conditions. A very complex model can fit observed data closely yet perform poorly on new cases. A simpler model can be preferable when it captures the important pattern and is easier to interpret.',
  'Feature extraction and classification are common analytical tasks. Feature extraction reduces complex observations to informative characteristics; for example, a signal may contain peaks or patterns relevant to the research question. Classification assigns observations to categories based on selected features or rules. Evaluate whether the extracted features are meaningful and whether classification performance is assessed fairly. Fault detection similarly seeks deviations from expected behavior; false alarms and missed faults both matter in interpreting system performance.'
 ]},
 {title:'5. Interpretation, uncertainty and communication',points:[
  'A result should be interpreted in relation to the original question, not in isolation. Explain the pattern in the data, the analysis that produced the result, and the assumptions that support the interpretation. Distinguish association from causation. A relationship between variables does not establish that one variable caused the other unless the study design and evidence support that inference and alternative explanations have been considered.',
  'Variability is part of data, not merely a defect. Some variation comes from random measurement or process effects; other variation may arise from systematic changes in conditions, instruments or populations. Quantify variation where possible and report it in a way that matches the analysis. If the data are noisy, conclusions may need to be more cautious. If the pattern is strong, it still needs to be judged against study limitations and possible bias.',
  'Communicate results with enough context to let readers evaluate them. State sample or data source, relevant units, analysis method and uncertainty. Tables and figures should agree with the text. Do not show only the result that supports a preferred explanation. Report contradictory, null or unexpected findings when they are relevant, and describe limitations that affect interpretation. Honest communication is a central research skill, not an optional final polish.'
 ]},
 {title:'6. Modelling skills as disciplined abstraction',points:[
  'Modelling requires deciding which features of a real system are important for a particular question and which can be simplified. A model is never the full system. Its assumptions should be explicit enough that another person can understand where the model may work and where it may fail. A useful model simplifies reasoning without hiding the very behavior the research is trying to explain.',
  'The process begins with a clear objective and a conceptual description of the system. Identify variables, inputs, outputs, constraints and relationships. Choose a mathematical, computational or conceptual form suited to the question, estimate parameters from appropriate evidence, and compare model implications with observations or independent knowledge. If the comparison is poor, investigate the assumptions, measurements and model structure rather than adjusting parameters blindly.',
  'Prediction and explanation should be distinguished in an exam answer. Prediction asks how well the model estimates outcomes, often for cases not used in model fitting. Explanation asks whether the proposed relationships provide a defensible account of how the system behaves. A model can be useful for one purpose and weak for another. State the validation evidence and the conditions to which the conclusion applies.'
 ]},
 {title:'7. Laboratory safety and responsible work',points:[
  'Laboratory safety begins before an experiment. Identify hazards associated with materials, equipment, energy sources and procedures. Review training, operating instructions, emergency arrangements and the protective measures required. Use approved procedures and do not work with unfamiliar equipment or materials without suitable guidance. A safe laboratory depends on individual care and shared systems for communicating hazards.',
  'Risk management is not the claim that all risk can be removed. It is the deliberate identification and control of risk so that work can proceed responsibly. Use appropriate protective equipment, maintain clear labels and records, keep the work area organized, and dispose of materials as instructed. Report incidents and near misses so that hazards can be addressed. Stop work when conditions differ materially from the approved procedure or when a safety control is unavailable.',
  'Safety also affects data quality. A rushed or improvised procedure can expose people to harm and produce unreliable measurements. Check equipment condition, calibration and setup; record deviations; and ensure that researchers know the steps they are expected to follow. In a written or oral exam, connect safety with planning, training, hazard recognition, controls, documentation and responsible reporting rather than reducing it to “wear protective gear.”'
 ]}
],
[
 {title:'1. Presentation, paper and audience',points:[
  'Researchers communicate through talks, technical presentations, reports and journal papers. These forms may use the same underlying research data but serve different communication conditions. A talk is delivered to a defined audience at a particular time. The speaker can respond to questions and adapt explanation to the audience’s background. A journal paper is read independently, possibly long after publication and by readers the author cannot identify or guide directly.',
  'A journal article is usually expected to follow a specific structure and style. The journal’s instructions govern details such as organization, references, figures and length. Papers are peer reviewed by independent experts who assess the work’s novelty, value and technical basis. Once published, the article becomes part of the durable record of the field. This permanence raises the importance of clear methods, accurate data and careful claims.',
  'A presentation needs a clear route through the material because listeners cannot easily reread a previous slide while the talk continues. A paper can include more detail and is available for repeated reading, but it must also be self-contained because the author is not present to explain it. Recognizing this difference helps researchers decide what to include in each format instead of transferring every paper paragraph onto slides.'
 ]},
 {title:'2. Plan the talk around a central message',points:[
  'Before preparing slides, identify the audience, purpose, time limit and the one central message listeners should remember. Then choose the evidence needed to support that message. A useful structure commonly introduces the problem and its importance, gives the approach, presents the key results and explains what they mean. The exact sequence may vary by field and talk type, but the audience should be able to see why each part follows the previous one.',
  'An outline slide can orient the audience and make the structure visible. The course presentation lecture recommends revisiting the outline when useful so listeners can recalibrate their understanding of where the talk is. Do not let the outline become a list of headings with no relationship to the argument. Each section should help answer the research question or establish context for the conclusion.',
  'Time is a design constraint. Select the most relevant results instead of attempting to show every measurement collected. A speaker should leave time to explain figures and conclusions. Rehearse aloud, check timing and anticipate questions. If an important detail cannot fit in the talk, it can be placed in a backup slide or paper, but the main claim should remain understandable without it.'
 ]},
 {title:'3. Use illustrations and figures well',points:[
  'Figures, diagrams and tables can make evidence easier to understand, but their form should match the information. A graph is useful for showing patterns and comparisons; a table may be clearer when exact values matter; a diagram can explain a process or apparatus. The choice should serve the audience’s task. Avoid visual decoration that competes with the scientific point or makes comparisons harder to judge.',
  'Every visual should be readable and explained. Use meaningful labels, units, legends and captions. Identify what the viewer should notice and state why it matters to the argument. Do not assume that the audience will infer the relation among axes, colors and results. If a figure contains several panels, explain how they relate and what conclusion can be drawn from them.',
  'Visuals should represent the data honestly. Use scales that do not exaggerate differences, disclose relevant uncertainty and avoid omitting inconvenient observations. Keep text large enough to read from the back of a room or on a standard display. In a paper, captions should help readers understand a figure independently; in a talk, the speaker supplies additional context verbally.'
 ]},
 {title:'4. Technical writing and paper structure',points:[
  'Technical writing presents a reasoned account of a problem, method, evidence and conclusion. The reader should be able to identify what question was asked, why it matters, what was done, what was observed and how the conclusion follows. Precision is more valuable than decorative complexity. Define terms that may be unfamiliar, use consistent names and units, and avoid sentences that hide who did what or what the evidence means.',
  'A research paper’s structure helps readers navigate a complex argument. The introduction places the problem in context and states the objective. Methods explain how the work was done at a level appropriate to the field. Results present observations; discussion interprets them in relation to the question and prior work. Conclusions summarize supported findings and their limits. Fields and journals vary, so follow the relevant style rather than forcing a universal template.',
  'Separate results from interpretation when that distinction helps clarity. A result might state that one measured quantity changed under specified conditions. The discussion can explain possible reasons, compare with prior studies and identify uncertainty. Avoid claiming more than the design supports. Clear writing helps reviewers and readers distinguish the evidence from the author’s interpretation.'
 ]},
 {title:'5. Citations, peer review and revision',points:[
  'Citations identify prior work and let readers trace the evidence behind a statement. Cite the source of borrowed ideas, methods, data and wording according to the required style. A reference list should contain complete, accurate details. Reference managers can help format bibliographies, but imported records and generated citations must be checked. Formatting consistency does not replace careful attribution.',
  'Peer review brings independent expert evaluation before publication. Reviewers may ask whether the question is meaningful, whether methods support the conclusion, whether the work is sufficiently original and whether the manuscript is clear. Review is a quality-control process, not a guarantee that a paper is error-free or universally accepted. Authors should respond to critiques with evidence and reasoned explanation, and revise where the critique reveals a genuine weakness.',
  'Revision should occur at several levels. First check the logic and organization: does the question lead to the method, and do the findings support the conclusion? Then examine paragraph flow, sentence clarity, terminology, figures, units and citations. Finally proofread for errors and confirm journal requirements. Editing only grammar cannot repair a confused argument, so revise the structure before polishing sentences.'
 ]},
 {title:'6. Exam framing and common mistakes',points:[
  'For a compare-and-contrast question about a presentation and paper, state the differences in audience, interaction, permanence, detail and structure. A presentation is interactive and time-limited; a paper is a durable record read independently and commonly peer reviewed. Both should communicate a coherent research argument and present evidence accurately.',
  'For a question about slide design, discuss audience, outline, visual selection, readability, explanation and summary. For technical writing, discuss precise language, logical sections, evidence, citations, journal style and revision. Apply the principles to a short scenario, such as improving a crowded slide or a paper claim unsupported by its results.',
  'Frequent mistakes include placing paragraphs on slides, using unreadable figures, presenting results without explaining their meaning, reading slides verbatim, ignoring the time limit, using inconsistent terminology, and making conclusions broader than the evidence. A strong communicator selects information for the format and makes the reasoning visible without concealing uncertainty.'
 ]}
],
[
 {title:'1. Purpose and scope of a literature survey',points:[
  'A literature survey gives the researcher an informed view of how a topic has developed. The course explains that no individual scientist can repeat the entire history of a field from the beginning. Instead, researchers build on work already done. A survey identifies the approaches others have tried, what those approaches established, where findings agree or conflict, and what questions remain. It provides background knowledge and helps the researcher locate a proposed study within the existing body of work.',
  'The survey has several practical purposes. It helps avoid unnecessary duplication, supports a claim that a proposed contribution is new, identifies useful methods, reveals controversies and points to gaps worth investigating. It also helps explain why a project matters to peers. A study is easier to evaluate when its relationship to current knowledge is clear. The survey should therefore be selective and analytical: it should include sources relevant to the question and explain why they matter rather than collect citations without a purpose.',
  'Literature review is not completed once at the beginning. New papers appear, new methods are developed and the researcher’s own understanding changes. Revisit the literature during the project, particularly before finalizing interpretations or making claims about novelty. When a result appears to conflict with published work, compare contexts, assumptions and methods before deciding that either result is wrong. The survey provides a basis for that comparison.'
 ]},
 {title:'2. Designing a search and finding sources',points:[
  'Start by turning the broad subject into a searchable question or set of concepts. Identify the main terms, synonyms, spelling variations and related phrases used in the field. Combine terms thoughtfully and refine the search after seeing the first results. A search that is too broad may return an unmanageable number of papers; one that is too narrow may miss relevant work because authors use different terminology. Record important search terms and decisions so that the process can be repeated or updated.',
  'The course demonstrates literature discovery through Web of Science and Scopus. These are scholarly indexing and search platforms that can help locate publications and follow connections among papers. Access may depend on institutional subscriptions. If a portal asks for a login and does not permit searching, check with the university library or research office rather than assuming the database has no content. Open-access sources and institutional access arrangements can supplement the search, but each source still needs to be evaluated for relevance and quality.',
  'Search results are starting points, not verified conclusions. Read titles and abstracts to screen for fit, then examine full papers that are central to the question. Check publication context, methods, sample or study conditions, limitations and references. Follow references backward to find foundational work and forward to find later studies that cite it. Keep enough information to retrieve each source again. Search software can organize results, but it cannot make the intellectual decision about whether a paper answers the question.'
 ]},
 {title:'3. Screening and evaluating literature',points:[
  'Set inclusion and exclusion criteria that match the question. Criteria might concern subject matter, study type, date range, population, material, method or language. The exact criteria depend on the project, but stating them reduces arbitrary selection. If the project is a broad narrative survey rather than a formal systematic review, be clear about the scope and limits of the search. Do not imply that a search was exhaustive if it was not.',
  'Evaluate each source by asking what question it addresses, what evidence it uses, how the method works and what conclusions are supported. Consider whether the assumptions fit the study context and whether the reported limitations affect your own question. A highly cited paper can be influential without being definitive. A newer paper can offer a useful result without resolving every issue. Compare sources instead of treating publication as automatic proof.',
  'A strong survey synthesizes. Organize papers into themes, methods, results or debates, then explain relationships among them. Identify consistent findings and disagreements. If results differ, look for differences in measurement, sample, conditions, assumptions or analysis. Explain what the collection of work tells you and what remains uncertain. The reader should be able to see how the research question follows from the review rather than encounter a disconnected list of summaries.'
 ]},
 {title:'4. From survey to research question',points:[
  'A gap is a reasoned conclusion from the survey, not merely an empty topic heading. It may be an unanswered question, a limitation in current evidence, an unexplained inconsistency, a method that needs validation or a context where existing results may not hold. Describe the evidence for the gap and its importance. A clear gap helps narrow a project from a broad area into work that can be completed and evaluated.',
  'Use the survey to define key terms and boundaries. Explain what the project will study and what it will not study. A project can become unmanageable if it attempts to answer every question raised by the literature. Prioritize the gap that best matches the time, resources, available expertise and intended contribution. A well-bounded question can still be significant if it is answered carefully.',
  'The review should support the choice of method. Prior studies may show that a measurement technique is accepted, reveal known limitations or suggest a comparison worth making. The researcher should not simply copy a prior method; they should explain why it is appropriate for the present question and whether any changes are needed. This connection among literature, question and method is a common way examiners assess whether a student understands research planning.'
 ]},
 {title:'5. Writing up and managing references',points:[
  'Writing up the literature is an argument about the state of knowledge. Introduce the topic, organize the discussion into logical sections, compare evidence and lead the reader toward the gap and research question. Each paragraph should have a point. A paragraph that only names one paper after another usually provides less value than a paragraph that compares several papers around a shared issue.',
  'Citations show which claims and ideas come from other sources and let readers locate the underlying evidence. Record author names, title, publication, year, page or identifier and other required details when the source is first collected. Use a consistent citation style and check each reference against the original publication. A citation manager or word-processing bibliography tool can reduce repetitive formatting, but it cannot guarantee correct metadata or appropriate citation placement.',
  'The course includes tutorials using Microsoft Word bibliographic sources and EndNote entries. The transferable lesson is to maintain a reliable source library and generate references consistently. Still review the final document: imported records can contain spelling errors, incomplete fields or incorrect formats. Attribute borrowed ideas even when paraphrased. Quotation marks and page locators may be required for direct quotations depending on the chosen style.'
 ]},
 {title:'6. Experimental skills and research records',points:[
  'Experimental work should be planned in relation to the question. Identify what needs to be observed, what equipment or materials are needed, what settings will be varied and what records will make the work interpretable. Review the procedure before starting and confirm that the researcher has appropriate training. When a result is unexpected, detailed records of setup, conditions and deviations help distinguish a meaningful observation from a problem in execution.',
  'A research record should be made close to the time the work occurs. Record dates, instrument settings, sample identifiers, units, calibration or preparation details, steps taken and any changes from the intended procedure. Keep raw observations distinct from processed or corrected values. This practice supports reproducibility, helps collaborators understand the work and makes later analysis more reliable.',
  'The experimental-skills topic belongs with literature review because methods are learned from prior work but must be adapted and justified. A paper may suggest a technique; practical training determines how to use it safely and accurately. The researcher should document why a particular approach was selected, what limitations it has and how those limitations affect interpretation. Good experimental practice begins before data collection and continues through reporting.'
 ]},
 {title:'7. Exam application and common errors',points:[
  'For an exam question about the purpose of a literature survey, explain at least four linked functions: background, novelty, avoiding duplication, identifying gaps or disagreements, selecting methods and placing work in context. Then apply these functions to a specific research topic. Avoid the weak answer that says only “to collect information.”',
  'For a search-method question, describe a repeatable sequence: define concepts, choose terms, search appropriate databases, screen results, inspect relevant full texts, follow references, record sources and synthesize findings. Mention access arrangements when relevant. For a question about writing, distinguish synthesis from a paper-by-paper catalogue and explain the role of accurate citations.',
  'Common mistakes include relying only on general web search, stopping after a few papers, treating citation counts as a quality guarantee, claiming a gap without evidence, copying wording without attribution, and trusting reference-manager output without checking it. A strong answer acknowledges scope and explains how the literature review will continue to inform the research as the project develops.'
 ]}
]
];

chapterStudyNotes.push(
[
 {title:'1. Creativity as disciplined exploration',points:[
  'Creativity in research is the ability to notice useful questions, connections or approaches and develop them into work that can be investigated. It is not a substitute for knowledge or evidence. Researchers often become creative by learning a topic deeply enough to see what is familiar, what is assumed and where a different approach might be useful. The course’s creativity lectures use examples and discussion to show that ideas may emerge through sustained attention rather than a single moment of inspiration.',
  'A creative contribution may arise when a familiar technique is used in a new area, when a new technique is applied to a familiar problem, or when ideas from separate domains are connected. The value of a new direction depends on the field and the problem. A surprising idea is not automatically a useful research idea; it must be compared with prior work, made specific and examined with suitable evidence.',
  'Researchers can make space for creativity by discussing ideas, reading beyond a narrow list, observing anomalies and allowing exploratory thinking before evaluating feasibility. They must also return to disciplined reasoning. Ask what question an idea answers, what evidence would support it, what assumptions it makes and what risks or resources it involves. This combination of openness and evaluation prevents creativity from becoming untestable speculation.'
 ]},
 {title:'2. From idea to researchable problem',points:[
  'A broad interest becomes a research problem when the researcher defines the subject, scope, context and question. “Study energy” is not yet a workable problem; the researcher must determine which system, condition, population or mechanism is of interest and what is unknown. Literature review is essential at this point because it distinguishes a genuinely open question from one that has already been answered and helps reveal the methods available.',
  'A research question should be clear enough to guide choices. It should indicate what will be investigated and, where useful, the relationship or difference being examined. Avoid including several unrelated problems in one question. A question can be revised as the researcher learns more, but revisions should follow evidence and be recorded. The final question and objectives should remain consistent with the method and the claims made in the conclusion.',
  'Feasibility matters. Consider time, equipment, expertise, access to data or participants, funding and safety. A highly ambitious question may need to be divided into stages or narrowed to a defensible part. Narrowing a topic is not necessarily reducing its value; focused work can produce a clearer contribution and stronger evidence than a project that tries to do too much.'
 ]},
 {title:'3. Ethics as a foundation for trustworthy research',points:[
  'The ethics discussion connects the word’s association with character to moral integrity in research. Research depends on trust: other researchers, institutions, participants and the public rely on the work being conducted and reported responsibly. Ethical conduct therefore supports the reliability and validity of findings. It is not just a final form or approval step, and it cannot be delegated entirely to a supervisor or committee.',
  'Ethical judgment begins with the choice of problem and continues through the conduct of the work. Researchers must consider how a study affects people, communities, animals, collaborators, colleagues and shared resources. The exact protections differ by research setting. When people are involved, respect, informed participation, privacy and appropriate review are central considerations. Follow the applicable institutional and professional requirements before the work begins.',
  'Integrity requires truthful reporting of what was planned and done, what the results show, and what remains uncertain. Do not fabricate or alter evidence to fit expectations, hide a relevant limitation, or present another person’s work as your own. Keep records that support the research account. If an error is discovered, respond openly and correct the record through appropriate channels. Trust is maintained by responsible decisions, especially when those decisions are inconvenient.'
 ]},
 {title:'4. Collaboration, authorship and sharing',points:[
  'Modern research is often collaborative. Collaboration raises questions about roles, access to shared equipment or data, decision-making and how contributions will be recognized. Discuss expectations early and revisit them if the work changes. Clear communication can prevent disputes and ensure that each person understands responsibilities for data, methods, analysis and publication.',
  'Authorship should reflect actual scholarly contributions and comply with the norms of the discipline and publisher. Being senior, funding the work or providing a resource does not automatically answer every authorship question. Keep a record of contributions and acknowledge assistance that does not qualify for authorship as appropriate. All listed authors share responsibilities for the work and should review the final manuscript and its claims.',
  'The course ethics discussion also raises willingness to share benefits. Research can create intellectual, social and financial value. Consider the interests of participants, collaborators, institutions and other stakeholders when results, publications or intellectual property are shared. Fairness does not always mean identical treatment; the form of sharing depends on the role and context. Ethical decisions should be explained and consistent with commitments made at the start.'
 ]},
 {title:'5. Risk management and responsibility',points:[
  'Research can involve risks to participants, researchers, communities, environments or property. The aim is not to claim that every risk can be eliminated, but to recognize risks and manage them. Identify what could go wrong, how likely and severe the consequences might be, who could be affected and what safeguards are available. Reassess the risk when the protocol, equipment, data or conditions change.',
  'A responsible researcher seeks required review and approval, follows the approved process, trains the research team and monitors whether safeguards are working. Participants should receive appropriate information and protection. In laboratory work, hazards and emergency procedures must be understood. In data research, privacy, security and access require attention. In field research, local context and impacts matter. The controls differ, but the obligation to anticipate and respond is shared.',
  'Responsibility also means acting when something goes wrong. Report incidents, errors, misconduct concerns or unexpected harms through appropriate channels. Do not conceal a problem because it may delay publication or damage a preferred result. Document actions taken and protect affected people. Prompt, transparent responses can limit harm and help preserve confidence in the research process.'
 ]},
 {title:'6. Exam application: ethical cases',points:[
  'In an ethics scenario, identify the stakeholders, the decision being made, possible harms and benefits, relevant duties or rules, and the evidence needed to decide. Then recommend a responsible course of action and explain how it will be documented. Do not rely on a vague statement such as “be honest”; show what honesty requires in that case—for example, preserving original measurements, reporting an unexpected outcome or correcting an authorship list.',
  'For a creativity question, connect novelty with prior knowledge and a researchable problem. Explain how a new combination or application can be valuable, then describe how the proposal can be assessed for feasibility, safety and evidence. Creativity and ethics are related: an innovative method still needs to respect participants, colleagues, law and scientific integrity.',
  'Common examination errors include treating ethics as only plagiarism, assuming approval makes every later action ethical, hiding inconvenient findings, overlooking collaborator contributions, and describing risk management as risk elimination. Strong answers recognize that responsibility continues throughout the study and that public trust depends on reliable, transparent work.'
 ]}
],
[
 {title:'1. Intellectual property and intellectual property rights',points:[
  'Intellectual property, or IP, refers to creations that arise from human intellectual or creative effort. Intellectual property rights, or IPR, are legal protections that may apply to specified kinds of creations. The course distinguishes the object of protection from the right associated with it. In casual discussion the expressions may be used interchangeably, but for analysis it is useful to ask two separate questions: what has been created, and what legal entitlement applies to it?',
  'A legal right is an entitlement recognized by law. Depending on its nature, it may give a person liberty to act, permission or a license to act, or the ability to exclude others from specified uses. Property rights concern things that can be possessed, owned or transferred; other rights may be inherent or shared more generally. Intellectual property rights are created and defined through legal frameworks, so their scope is not simply determined by who first says an idea aloud.',
  'IP matters in research because projects can create inventions, written works, software, designs, datasets, methods and other outputs. Not every output is protected in the same way, and some ideas or results may not meet requirements for a particular right. Avoid assuming that an institution, student, funder or collaborator automatically owns everything. Ownership and use depend on applicable law, agreements, employment or student status and institutional rules.'
 ]},
 {title:'2. Patents and copyright: distinguish the examples',points:[
  'The course gives an invention as an example of intellectual property that may be protected by a patent. A patent is a legal right concerning an eligible invention and is subject to formal requirements. It is not a general certificate that an idea is valuable, nor does every experimental result qualify. The creator or research team should seek suitable advice to understand eligibility, filing, ownership and disclosure issues in the relevant jurisdiction.',
  'A book or original expression in words is used as an example associated with copyright. Copyright protects particular forms of expression under applicable law, rather than granting ownership of every underlying fact or idea. Researchers should therefore distinguish the idea or method from the specific text, figure or other expression in which it appears. Permissions and licenses can affect how a protected work may be reused.',
  'These examples illustrate why the form of an output matters. An invention, a manuscript, a software program and a logo may raise different IP questions. One output can also involve several rights or agreements. An examination answer should avoid saying “IP means patent” or “copyright protects every idea.” Give the relevant kind of creation and explain the corresponding type of protection at a general level.'
 ]},
 {title:'3. Rights, ownership and exclusivity',points:[
  'The lecture explains that rights can have different functions. Some rights describe a freedom or liberty; some confer permission or a license. Rights can be inherent to a person or attached to property through legal recognition. Certain rights are general and shared, while others are exclusive. An exclusive right can permit its holder to stop others from specified acts involving the protected subject matter.',
  'Exclusivity is not unlimited ownership of all possible uses. A right has a defined subject, scope, duration and set of exceptions or conditions. These limits matter in research because access to an output does not always mean permission to reproduce, distribute or commercialize it. Similarly, placing something online does not necessarily put it in the public domain. Check the license, agreement and applicable policy before use.',
  'Ownership may be affected by multiple contributors and institutional or funding arrangements. A research output can involve student work, supervisor input, laboratory resources, employer obligations and external funding. Discuss ownership and intended use before disputes arise. Keep a clear record of who contributed and what agreements apply. When in doubt, seek advice from the institution’s technology transfer or IP office.'
 ]},
 {title:'4. IP decisions across the research lifecycle',points:[
  'Consider IP at the planning stage. Identify background materials, software, instruments, datasets and licenses that the project will use. Determine whether collaborators or sponsors have restrictions on access, publication or ownership. This groundwork reduces the risk of discovering late in the project that an important resource cannot be used as expected or that publication timing conflicts with a potential protection strategy.',
  'During the work, keep dated records of conception, development, experiments, versions and contributions. Store confidential information appropriately and follow agreements governing disclosure. If a potentially protectable invention or design emerges, contact the appropriate institutional office before public release. Conference slides, preprints, theses, posters, websites and informal demonstrations may count as disclosure in some contexts, so do not assume that only a journal article matters.',
  'When communicating or transferring an output, clarify whether a license, permission, confidentiality agreement or other arrangement is required. A license grants specified permission; it is not necessarily a transfer of ownership. Use only the rights needed, respect third-party materials and maintain attribution. Ethical scholarship and IP compliance work together: researchers must credit sources while also checking legal permission where attribution alone is insufficient.'
 ]},
 {title:'5. IP, open research and academic communication',points:[
  'Academic research values publication and exchange, while IP systems can provide control over certain uses. These goals need not always conflict. Researchers can publish, share data or release software under defined licenses, while preserving specific rights or observing disclosure constraints. The appropriate path depends on the output, collaborators, sponsor, institution and intended public benefit.',
  'Open access to a paper does not automatically mean that every reuse is allowed without conditions. Check the license and publisher terms. A paper can be freely readable but still require attribution or permission for certain reproductions. The same distinction applies to images, code, datasets and teaching materials. Record licenses when downloading resources so that future reuse is informed.',
  'In collaborative work, discuss how results will be shared and how authorship, inventorship, credit and financial benefits may be handled. These concepts are related but not interchangeable. Authorship recognizes scholarly contribution to a publication; inventorship is a specific legal concept related to a patent claim. A person may be an author without being an inventor, or vice versa, depending on the facts and applicable rules.'
 ]},
 {title:'6. Exam approach and practical checklist',points:[
  'For a definition question, state that IP concerns human creations and IPR are legally recognized protections associated with certain creations. For an example question, distinguish an invention and patent from a literary work and copyright. Explain rights as legal entitlements that can include exclusive control over specified uses, not as unrestricted ownership of all ideas.',
  'For a scenario involving public presentation of an invention, identify the possible disclosure issue, the relevant contributors and institution, and the need for prompt advice before publication or demonstration. For a scenario involving reuse of a figure, identify copyright or license terms and explain that citation alone may not grant permission. Apply course concepts without attempting to give a legal ruling beyond the facts.',
  'A responsible researcher asks: Who contributed? What agreements apply? What is the output and what rights may protect it? Is any material owned by another party? What can be disclosed now? Which office can advise? How should the output be licensed or credited? This sequence helps connect IP theory to practical decisions while respecting the limits of a general course introduction.'
 ]}
],
[
 {title:'1. What design of experiments is for',points:[
  'Design of Experiments (DoE) is a structured way to plan experiments so that changes in input conditions can be related to measured responses. Rather than changing one setting informally and hoping to learn something, the researcher defines the objective, chooses relevant factors and levels, measures a response and selects a sequence of runs that can answer the question. The course presents DoE as a way to understand which variables influence a process and to explore conditions that may improve an outcome.',
  'The response is the outcome measured in an experiment, such as yield, strength, temperature or processing time. Factors are inputs or conditions that may influence the response. A level is a chosen setting or value of a factor. A nuisance variable may affect results even though it is not the focus of the study. Clear definitions are essential because an experiment cannot be interpreted if the researcher does not know which conditions changed and what was measured.',
  'DoE can support screening, estimation of effects, model development and optimization. These are related but distinct goals. Screening identifies which factors appear influential. Estimating effects quantifies how responses change with factors. Model development represents relationships among factors and responses. Optimization searches for conditions that best meet an objective subject to constraints. State which goal the design serves before selecting runs.'
 ]},
 {title:'2. Variation and repeated experiments',points:[
  'The same nominal experiment may not produce an identical response each time. The course emphasizes that scatter is expected because random influences cannot all be controlled. An observed response can be thought of as a systematic component plus a random error component. The systematic component reflects the true response associated with the conditions; random error shifts the observed value above or below that response. The true component is not directly known, so it must be estimated from experiments.',
  'Variation should be quantified and reported rather than treated as proof that the work is bad. Repeated observations help reveal how much responses vary under similar conditions. An average summarizes a group but does not describe its spread, so variability measures and visual displays can also matter. Interpret a difference in means in light of the amount of scatter and the number of observations, not simply by comparing two isolated values.',
  'Separate random variation from systematic variation. Random variation produces unpredictable scatter around an underlying response. Systematic variation arises when a factor, time, instrument, operator or other condition changes in a structured way. A design should make it possible to distinguish the effect of the factor of interest from other sources of variation. If these sources are confounded, the researcher may not know what caused the observed change.'
 ]},
 {title:'3. Screening factors and planning runs',points:[
  'When a process has many possible input variables, testing every combination may be expensive or impractical. Screening experiments are used to identify factors that appear to have meaningful effects on the response. Factors with little observed influence within the studied range may be held fixed in later experiments, reducing the number of conditions that need to be studied. Screening is a first step, not proof that a factor can never matter under any conditions.',
  'Plan factor ranges carefully. Levels should be safe, feasible and relevant to the process. A range that is too narrow may hide an effect; a range that is too broad may cross into unrealistic or unsafe conditions. Record the settings and order of runs. If experimental conditions drift over time, account for that possibility through appropriate planning and analysis.',
  'Once influential factors are identified, plan follow-up runs to learn more about their effects and interactions. The next experiment should be chosen for the information it can provide or the progress it can make toward the objective. Avoid deciding after the fact which runs to keep because their results are convenient. A planned sequence protects the interpretation from selective choices.'
 ]},
 {title:'4. Randomization, replication and blocking',points:[
  'Replication means repeating experimental conditions so that variability can be estimated and repeatability can be assessed. A repeated run is not redundant if it provides information about noise and confidence in the response. Replication does not eliminate bias or guarantee reproducibility in a different setting, but it gives a basis for understanding within-study variation.',
  'Randomization changes the order in which experimental runs are performed. It helps prevent an unrecognized time trend or external influence from being systematically associated with one factor level. Randomization should be implemented and recorded rather than invoked vaguely. If there are constraints on run order, explain them and consider how they might affect the analysis.',
  'Blocking groups runs that share a known nuisance condition, such as a day, batch or instrument, so that comparisons can account for that source of variation. The factor of primary interest should not be confused with the block. Replication, randomization and blocking address different design concerns; they can be combined where appropriate, but none replaces a clear research question or sound measurement.'
 ]},
 {title:'5. Analysis and statistical reasoning',points:[
  'After data collection, examine the response values and the variation before interpreting effects. Use suitable plots and summaries to understand patterns. Compare factor settings using an analysis that matches the design. If a model is fitted, check whether its assumptions and residual behavior are reasonable. Report estimates of effects and uncertainty, not just a statement that one condition “won.”',
  'The course introduces statistical concepts that support DoE, including random variables, probability distributions, significance levels, p-values, confidence intervals and mean square error. A random variable represents a quantity whose value is uncertain before observation; a realized observation is one value. A probability distribution describes the behavior expected across possible outcomes. These concepts help researchers reason about scatter and inference.',
  'A p-value is calculated under a specified null model and assumptions. It measures how incompatible the observed data are with that model, not the probability that the null hypothesis is true. A confidence interval expresses uncertainty around an estimated quantity under the method’s assumptions. Mean square error is one measure used in statistical analysis of variation. In exams, define terms in context and avoid treating a threshold as a complete scientific conclusion.'
 ]},
 {title:'6. Optimization and confirmation',points:[
  'Optimization depends on the objective. A researcher may seek maximum yield, minimum energy use, a target value, or a compromise among several responses. Constraints such as safety, cost, equipment capability and product requirements define which settings are acceptable. The best setting is therefore best relative to a stated objective and feasible region, not in the abstract.',
  'The design space is the range of factor combinations that has been considered. Do not claim that an optimum is global if only a limited region has been tested. A fitted response model can guide the next experiments, but predictions should be treated cautiously outside the observed range. Use follow-up runs to check that a proposed optimum produces the expected response under practical conditions.',
  'Confirmation experiments provide a direct check of a predicted or selected condition. Compare observed results with the model’s prediction and uncertainty. If the result differs, examine model assumptions, uncontrolled variables and measurement quality, then update the model or design. Optimization is an evidence-driven sequence rather than a single mathematical answer detached from the experiment.'
 ]},
 {title:'7. Exam structure for a DoE problem',points:[
  'For a design question, begin by stating the objective and response. Identify each factor, its levels and any important constraints. Explain the proposed runs and why they provide information about the question. Describe how you will handle random variability, replication and any nuisance factors. Then state how the data will be analyzed and what a result would allow you to conclude.',
  'If asked why repeated runs differ, explain the role of random error and unavoidable variability. If asked about screening, state that it identifies likely influential factors so later work can focus on them. If asked about optimization, identify the objective and constraints, use an appropriate design to explore the response, and confirm the selected conditions.',
  'Common mistakes include changing several settings without recording them, ignoring scatter, confusing replication with merely taking many measurements from one run, interpreting significance as practical importance, and claiming causation from a design that cannot separate factors. A strong answer makes the route from question to design to inference explicit.'
 ]}
],
[
 {title:'1. Research differs across disciplines',points:[
  'The final course week presents research across many areas, including applied mechanics, chemical engineering, civil engineering, computer science and engineering, engineering design, humanities and social sciences, mechanical engineering, metallurgical and materials engineering, ocean engineering, management studies, aerospace engineering, biotechnology, chemistry, electrical engineering, mathematics and physics. This range illustrates the breadth of research and the different kinds of questions that can be studied.',
  'Disciplines differ in their objects of study, assumptions, evidence and methods. An experimental laboratory study may rely on controlled conditions and instruments; a social-science study may use interviews, surveys or documents; mathematics may develop and prove formal results; design research may evaluate artifacts and processes. These examples are broad, not rigid definitions. Many real projects combine approaches, and each method must be justified by the question and disciplinary standards.',
  'The course does not suggest that one discipline’s methods can be transferred without thought. A technique useful in one field may need adaptation, validation or a different interpretation in another. When comparing fields, identify the research problem, type of evidence and method rather than merely naming the department. Interdisciplinary research requires researchers to make assumptions and terminology clear across areas.'
 ]},
 {title:'2. Open-ended problems and assumptions',points:[
  'Classroom exercises often present a well-defined problem with enough information to calculate an answer. Research questions are more open-ended. A researcher may know only part of what is needed and must decide how to define the problem, which variables or concepts matter, what evidence to collect and what assumptions are defensible. This is a major transition described by faculty and scholars in the course.',
  'Assumptions are unavoidable, but they should be explicit and justified. They help make a problem tractable and define what a result means. If an assumption is unrealistic or unsupported, the conclusion may not apply. Researchers should test important assumptions where possible, compare alternatives and explain limitations. An exam answer should not present assumptions as facts simply because a model or method requires them.',
  'Problem definition is itself a research skill. A broad topic must be narrowed into a question that can be investigated with available resources and a suitable method. The problem can develop as the scholar learns more, but it should not drift without reason. Keep a record of why the scope or assumptions changed and how that affects objectives and interpretation.'
 ]},
 {title:'3. Research across boundaries',points:[
  'Department labels do not always mark the limits of a research question. A scholar may work across materials, biotechnology, mechanical engineering, computing, management or social science when the problem requires combined expertise. The course scholar discussion notes that researchers can enter areas beyond their original degree background, provided they develop the required knowledge and work with appropriate guidance.',
  'Interdisciplinary work brings both opportunities and challenges. Different fields may use the same term in different ways, value different forms of evidence or have different standards for a convincing result. Teams should agree on definitions, roles, data practices and how methods will be integrated. A project is not interdisciplinary merely because several departments are named; the perspectives must contribute to the question or method.',
  'When working beyond a home discipline, identify what you already know and what expertise is missing. Literature review, coursework, discussion with specialists and collaboration can fill gaps. Do not overstate competence in a method that has not been learned. Interdisciplinary research is strongest when contributions are coordinated and the reasoning is understandable to all relevant audiences.'
 ]},
 {title:'4. Choosing an area, advisor and research path',points:[
  'The research-scholar discussion offers practical advice about choosing a topic and advisor. Sustained interest matters because a researcher spends a long time with the problem. Also consider whether the area has appropriate guidance, resources, a workable scope and an environment where you can learn. These factors should be weighed together rather than choosing only by reputation or by a vague promise of success.',
  'Students do not always need to arrive with a fully defined thesis problem. Some programs provide time for coursework and exploration before the project is settled. The amount of freedom and structure differs by advisor and program. Learn how a prospective advisor supervises, how often meetings occur, whether problem definition is collaborative and what support is available. Clear expectations can prevent avoidable frustration.',
  'A research path is uncertain, so career and practical goals should be considered thoughtfully without expecting a guaranteed outcome. Scholars in the course emphasize perseverance, patience, teamwork and adapting to failure. They also recommend thinking about the area and working relationship before joining. A good decision is an informed choice with a realistic view of both the intellectual interest and the conditions of the work.'
 ]},
 {title:'5. Research habits from scholar experiences',points:[
  'Research scholars describe learning through unsuccessful attempts. Experiments may not work, plans may change and results may take time. The useful response is to accept setbacks, diagnose them, learn from them and recognize partial progress. Perseverance does not mean ignoring evidence or repeating an ineffective action indefinitely; it means continuing with reflection and a willingness to adapt.',
  'Patience and collaboration matter alongside technical ability. Research often depends on other people’s expertise, shared equipment and careful teamwork. Understanding how colleagues feel, communicating clearly and respecting different backgrounds can improve the work. A researcher should seek help when appropriate and contribute reliably to a group.',
  'Regular routines support long-term inquiry. Keep notes, manage time, discuss progress and maintain enough structure to revisit decisions. A scholar’s confidence and skills can grow as the work proceeds. The course presents research as a process that develops the researcher, not only as a sequence of results. Reflecting on that process can help students make informed decisions about future study.'
 ]},
 {title:'6. Preparing for discipline-based questions',points:[
  'When asked to compare research in two fields, organize the answer around the question, evidence, method and constraints. For example, explain whether the work studies a material, a design, a social process or a formal system; describe what observations or reasoning are used; and identify why the method fits. Avoid unsupported stereotypes such as claiming that one field is purely theoretical or another is purely experimental.',
  'If asked what is common across disciplines, emphasize systematic inquiry: clear questions, justified methods, trustworthy evidence, interpretation, ethical practice and communication. If asked what differs, discuss the objects of study, data, tools, assumptions and criteria for validation. Shared principles do not erase disciplinary differences, and differences do not prevent useful collaboration.',
  'For a career or scholar-discussion question, connect open-endedness with learning, persistence, advisor interaction, teamwork and topic choice. Use examples from the course sessions when possible. A strong answer presents both the challenge and the way researchers respond, rather than giving a generic list of positive traits.'
 ]}
]
);

chapterStudyNotes[0].push({title:'7. Extended synthesis: from curiosity to defensible claim',points:[
 'A useful exam response makes the relationship among curiosity, prior knowledge and evidence explicit. Curiosity may begin a project, but a researchable contribution emerges only when the idea is compared with prior work and translated into a question. The question then constrains what evidence is relevant. If the evidence does not answer the question, a large volume of work does not automatically make the project strong. This is why problem definition and method selection deserve as much thought as the final result.',
 'When a researcher interprets a finding, ask three levels of questions: what was observed, what explanation is supported, and how far the explanation may generalize. These are separate claims. For example, a measured difference is an observation; attributing it to a mechanism is an interpretation; claiming the mechanism applies to other systems is a generalization. Each step needs additional reasoning and evidence. Writing these levels separately helps avoid overclaiming.',
 'An original contribution can be a new observation, method, explanation, dataset, comparison or application. Its value depends on the field and the question. State what is added, why the addition matters and what evidence establishes it. A contribution can be small but still useful if it is reliable and clearly situated. Conversely, ambitious language does not make a contribution important when the evidence is weak.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[1].push({title:'8. Extended synthesis: from database search to coherent review',points:[
 'A repeatable search process improves both coverage and credibility. Keep a short search log containing databases used, date searched, terms and combinations, filters and reasons for excluding central sources. The log does not need to be elaborate for every project, but it helps the researcher update the search and answer questions about scope. In a formal review, more structured reporting may be required; follow the method expected in the field.',
 'Do not confuse the number of papers with the quality of a literature review. A short, carefully selected group of foundational and directly relevant studies can support a clear argument better than a long list of loosely related titles. Include different perspectives when they matter, including studies that challenge the preferred explanation. This reduces confirmation bias and shows whether the proposed question remains open.',
 'Synthesis can be organized chronologically when the development of a field matters, thematically when several approaches address the same issue, or methodologically when techniques are being compared. Each section should explain the connection among studies and how it advances the argument. End by identifying the unresolved point and stating how the proposed project will address it. That transition is the logical bridge from review to research design.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[3].push({title:'8. Extended synthesis: measurement quality and the evidence trail',points:[
 'Measurement quality affects every later conclusion. Define each measured variable, use consistent units and document the instrument or source. If a value is derived from several measurements, preserve the calculation steps. If human judgment is involved, explain the coding or rating procedure and how consistency was considered. These details let readers judge whether the observed evidence corresponds to the concept named in the research question.',
 'Keep a traceable evidence trail from data to conclusion. Preserve original records, maintain a clean copy for analysis, document exclusions and transformations, and label derived datasets clearly. A figure should be reproducible from the recorded data and stated procedure. When multiple versions of analysis exist, retain notes about which one supports the reported result and why decisions changed.',
 'A strong analysis answer moves in order: identify the objective, describe the data and measurement, choose an appropriate method, report an estimate or pattern with uncertainty, check assumptions, and interpret within scope. This order prevents statistical technique from dominating the scientific question. It also makes it easier to distinguish a computation mistake from a conceptual mistake.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[2].push({title:'7. Extended synthesis: communication choices',points:[
 'Communication begins with selection. A researcher has more information than any single talk or paper can contain, so the task is to choose the information the audience needs to understand and assess the argument. Selection should not hide evidence that changes the interpretation. Instead, prioritize central results in the main narrative and place supporting detail where readers can find it.',
 'A talk is successful when listeners can state the question, main method, strongest evidence and conclusion after it ends. Test this by explaining the work to a colleague unfamiliar with the project. If the colleague cannot see why a result supports the conclusion, the sequence may need revision. A single summary slide can help connect the pieces, but only if it expresses their relation rather than repeating headings.',
 'For a manuscript, completeness matters because the reader cannot ask the author for missing steps. Include enough methodological detail for evaluation, define terms, cite relevant prior work and identify limitations. Completeness does not mean including every procedural detail in the main text; supplemental material may carry detail when the journal permits it. The standard is whether readers can understand and evaluate the claim.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[4].push({title:'7. Extended synthesis: responsible decisions in difficult cases',points:[
 'Ethical cases often involve competing values rather than a simple rule lookup. A researcher may need to balance participant privacy with transparent data sharing, publication timing with a possible IP disclosure, or individual credit with a team outcome. Identify the affected parties and the commitments already made. Consult relevant institutional guidance and document the reasoning. The answer should protect people and preserve the integrity of the evidence while addressing the legitimate research objective.',
 'Conflicts of interest do not automatically prove misconduct, but they can affect judgment or public trust. Disclose relevant interests through required channels and follow management conditions. Researchers should not decide alone that a conflict is harmless when policy requires review. Transparent handling gives collaborators, institutions and readers a basis for evaluating the work.',
 'When errors occur, distinguish honest error from intentional fabrication, falsification or plagiarism. All errors should be addressed, but the response may differ depending on cause and impact. Preserve records, inform responsible parties and correct the scientific record when needed. Concealment usually compounds the original problem and undermines trust.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[5].push({title:'7. Extended synthesis: applying IP concepts to research outputs',points:[
 'Consider a project that produces a manuscript, a software tool, a laboratory device and a dataset. Each output may raise a different question. Copyright may be relevant to particular expression or code; patent eligibility may be relevant to an invention; data may be governed by privacy, contract or institutional rules; and software may carry an open-source license. The researcher should identify each output separately rather than ask only whether “the project” is owned.',
 'Background IP means material that existed before the project or is brought into it; project-generated IP arises through the work. Agreements can define how each may be used. If the project relies on third-party software or data, its terms may constrain distribution of a combined result. Keep an inventory of key inputs, owners, licenses and contributors so the team can make informed choices before release.',
 'Publication and protection decisions can have timing consequences. If a team may seek patent protection, public disclosure before filing can affect available options under some legal systems. The safest general advice is to contact the institution’s IP office early, before a public talk, thesis upload, preprint or paper submission. This is a planning issue, not a reason to conceal research indefinitely; the team should align protection with the institution’s publication and public-benefit responsibilities.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[6].push({title:'8. Extended synthesis: design logic and defensible inference',points:[
 'An experiment is informative when the design allows the researcher to distinguish plausible explanations. If two factors always change together, their separate effects cannot be identified from the observed comparison. If measurements are taken only under one condition, there is no basis for estimating how a factor changes the response. Before running an experiment, ask which comparisons are needed and whether the planned runs create those comparisons.',
 'Replication estimates variation under repeated conditions, while randomization helps distribute hidden influences across the run order. Blocking handles a known source of variation by grouping comparable runs. These techniques solve different problems and should be selected based on the process. They do not compensate for an unclear objective, a biased measurement or an unsafe range of settings.',
 'Factor interactions are important when the effect of one variable depends on another. A one-factor-at-a-time strategy can miss such relationships because it does not test combinations systematically. A designed set of combinations can reveal whether a factor’s effect changes with another factor. The suitable design depends on the number of factors, resources, assumptions and objective; explain the logic rather than naming a design without justification.',
 'Statistical significance, practical importance and reproducibility are distinct. A small effect can be statistically detectable but irrelevant to the real objective. A large observed difference can be too uncertain to support a strong claim. A result may be repeatable within one setup yet fail in a different context. Discuss these dimensions separately when interpreting a designed experiment.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[7].push({title:'7. Extended synthesis: comparing fields without flattening differences',points:[
 'A useful comparison of disciplines identifies both a common research structure and field-specific standards. For example, all fields need a question and defensible evidence, but what counts as evidence differs. A mathematical proof, a materials test, an interview transcript and an engineering prototype are not interchangeable. Each must be judged by appropriate standards and connected to the claim it is intended to support.',
 'Disciplinary perspectives influence which problems are considered important and which methods are available. A researcher entering a new area should learn its vocabulary, foundational literature, accepted practices and debates. This avoids importing assumptions that make sense in the home field but not in the new context. Consultation with people who know the discipline is a strength, not a weakness.',
 'Interdisciplinary teams can make complementary methods useful, but they need integration. Decide how findings from one method will inform another, how disagreements will be resolved and what level of evidence supports a combined conclusion. If disciplines reach different interpretations, describe the difference rather than forcing false agreement. The goal is a coherent answer to the problem, not merely a collection of methods.',
 'The course’s broad range of departments also reminds students that research direction can evolve. A person may bring skills from one field to a new topic, but they must build the missing expertise and recognize limits. A calculated choice of area, guide and resources can make the transition manageable. Patience and openness support this learning process.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[1].push({title:'8. Connecting analysis to a research question',points:[
 'A data-analysis plan should be stated before looking for a preferred result. Identify the outcome and explanatory variables, the population or process of interest, and the kind of conclusion sought. Then choose summaries, plots and models that match those needs. If the analysis changes after initial inspection, explain why; exploratory analysis can guide further work, but confirmatory claims should be distinguished from patterns discovered after examining the same data.',
 'The course notes that the procedures and interpretations must vary with the application. A sensor stream, a survey, an image and a laboratory measurement have different data structures and sources of error. Apply methods with awareness of how observations were produced. A statistical technique cannot correct a mismatch between the measurement and the concept or remove bias introduced before analysis.',
 'When reporting an analysis, show enough information for a reader to understand the data, method, result and uncertainty. State units and relevant sample details, define transformations and explain exclusions. A numerical answer without context is not a research conclusion. Explain what the result means for the question and what it does not establish.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[4].push({title:'8. Evaluating a creative proposal',points:[
 'A creative proposal can be assessed by asking whether it addresses an important question, whether the proposed novelty is supported by a literature survey, and whether the method can produce evidence relevant to the claim. Consider feasibility, skills, time, access and safety. The proposal should identify a plausible contribution and explain what result would count as progress, including the possibility that the initial expectation is not supported.',
 'Research creativity can be individual or collaborative. Discussion with people who have different expertise may reveal a connection that one researcher misses. Collaboration should be organized so that responsibilities, credit, data handling and publication expectations are clear. An idea should not be treated as the property of whoever spoke first without considering the contributions and institutional arrangements.',
 'A creative result may be valuable even when it is not immediately useful in a commercial sense. Its contribution could clarify a mechanism, introduce a method, resolve a disagreement or open a path for later work. Explain the value in terms of the research community or affected stakeholders, and do not promise outcomes that the evidence cannot guarantee.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[5].push({title:'8. Distinguishing legal protection from academic credit',points:[
 'Academic citation gives credit and enables readers to trace sources, but it does not itself transfer a legal right or provide permission to reuse protected material. Conversely, a license may permit reuse under conditions while attribution remains academically important. Consider both dimensions when using another person’s text, image, code, invention or data.',
 'Inventorship and authorship answer different questions. Inventorship is determined by legal criteria connected to the claims of a patent; authorship reflects scholarly contribution to a publication and publisher or disciplinary rules. Teams should not assume the lists are identical. Ask the relevant office or publisher for guidance when roles are unclear.',
 'A researcher should preserve evidence of contributions and agreements, disclose relevant conflicts and follow institutional policies. Do not sign a transfer or license without understanding the rights and obligations it creates. In an exam, keep explanations general: identify the distinction and the need to consult governing rules rather than inventing jurisdiction-specific legal requirements.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[7].push({title:'8. Turning a discipline example into an exam answer',points:[
 'The course’s department sessions can be used as examples of how research questions vary. Applied mechanics may examine motion or forces; materials research may study structure and properties; computer science may investigate algorithms or systems; social sciences may study people and institutions; mathematics may develop formal results. These are illustrative associations rather than complete definitions of each field. Explain the actual example you choose and avoid treating a department label as a method.',
 'A useful short answer names the field, describes a question or problem, identifies the evidence or method and states what the work seeks to understand. Then compare it with another field using the same dimensions. This method produces a meaningful comparison instead of a list of department names.',
 'When asked about research scholars’ advice, use concrete themes from the discussion: perseverance through failed attempts, patience, collaboration, sustained interest, thoughtful selection of a topic and guide, and openness to interdisciplinary work. Explain why each matters to open-ended research. A developed answer links the advice to how researchers plan, communicate or revise their work.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[2].push({title:'8. A practical revision checklist for communication',points:[
 'Before a talk, test the opening: can a listener understand the problem and why it matters without already knowing the project? Test the transition between sections: does each slide prepare the next one? Test the ending: does the conclusion answer the stated question and remain within the evidence? These checks reveal structure problems that are easy to miss when a speaker knows the project too well.',
 'For a paper, check that the abstract and conclusion agree with the results, that each claim is supported by a cited source or reported observation, and that the methods explain the essential steps. Check that figure references point to the correct figure and that units, abbreviations and terminology remain consistent. A final read should ask whether a reader could reconstruct the argument, not only whether every sentence is grammatical.',
 'Adapt explanation to the audience without changing the evidence. A specialist audience may need technical detail; a broader audience may need terms defined and context made explicit. In either case, do not oversimplify a limitation into a false certainty. Accessibility and accuracy can coexist when the speaker chooses examples and wording carefully.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[3].push({title:'9. Maintaining a useful literature record',points:[
 'Keep search results organized so that sources can be retrieved during writing. A reference record should include enough bibliographic detail to identify the publication and, where useful, a short note on its question, approach and relevance. Separate your own comments from copied quotations. This reduces accidental plagiarism and makes synthesis faster when the review grows.',
 'When several papers support the same point, cite the sources that are most relevant and representative rather than adding a long citation string without explanation. When papers disagree, cite both sides and describe the basis of the disagreement. A literature review should represent the field fairly, including evidence that complicates the proposed explanation.',
 'Before submission, compare every in-text citation with the bibliography and every bibliography entry with the text or reference policy. Remove unused references if the style requires it and repair missing entries. Citation tools are conveniences, not authorities; consistency and traceability remain the author’s responsibility.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[4].push({title:'9. Ethical reasoning through the project',points:[
 'A project’s ethical profile can change. A participant may disclose a new risk, a data set may contain identifying information, a collaborator may leave, or the research question may shift. Reassess whether existing approvals and safeguards still apply. Continuing under an outdated plan can create harm even when the original design was responsibly approved.',
 'Research teams should create a culture where people can raise concerns without retaliation. Clear procedures for reporting, record keeping and review help ensure that concerns are handled fairly. Senior researchers carry particular responsibility because their decisions shape the environment for students and staff, but every team member must follow agreed practices and report serious issues.',
 'Ethical integrity includes how findings are communicated after the study. Explain limitations, disclose relevant interests, give proper credit and correct errors. If a result may affect policy, health, safety or public understanding, consider how uncertainty will be communicated. Responsible communication prevents audiences from treating tentative evidence as a certainty.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[5].push({title:'9. Practical distinctions for IP scenarios',points:[
 'If a researcher creates a figure using another author’s published data, there may be both citation and permission questions. If the figure is independently redrawn, it may still use protected expression or data subject to conditions. Check the source license and publisher rules rather than assuming that a new drawing removes all obligations.',
 'If a research team develops a device during a funded project, review the funding agreement, institutional rules and contributor record before deciding ownership or release. A student should not make assumptions based only on who performed the experiment. The appropriate office can interpret relevant policies and coordinate protection or publication decisions.',
 'If software is used or released, examine its license and dependencies. Reusing open-source code may be permitted under conditions that affect redistribution. Cite or acknowledge the source and comply with license terms. These examples show why IP awareness should be integrated into research planning, not postponed until a manuscript is accepted.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[6].push({title:'9. Design choices and limitations',points:[
 'No design can answer every possible question. A screening design may identify large effects but provide limited detail about curvature or interactions. A detailed response-surface study may require more runs. Choose the design by balancing information, cost, time, safety and precision. State what the design can and cannot establish.',
 'Measurement error can obscure factor effects. Calibrate or verify instruments, use consistent procedures and choose a response measure that reflects the objective. If measurement noise is large, more experimental runs alone may not solve the problem; improve the measurement process where possible.',
 'Report the tested factor region and conditions. A conclusion about an effect applies to the range and setup studied unless further evidence supports broader generalization. Replication or confirmation in a different batch, day or apparatus may be needed to assess robustness. Explicit limits make the result more useful because readers can judge where it applies.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[7].push({title:'9. Integrating the course perspectives',points:[
 'The discipline sessions fit together with the earlier course chapters. A researcher in any field must locate prior work, formulate a question, select a fitting method, analyze evidence, communicate clearly and act ethically. The technical details vary, but the logic of connecting question, method and evidence is shared. This is a useful framework for organizing the course as a whole.',
 'The scholar discussion makes clear that a research path is shaped by both intellectual choices and working conditions. Topic, advisor, collaborators, facilities, time and career goals all influence what can be done. Consider these factors thoughtfully, but remain open to learning; the precise problem may become clearer after entering a research program.',
 'A strong research identity combines specialist depth with broader judgment. Depth helps the researcher understand a particular problem, while broad learning makes it possible to communicate across disciplines and recognize connections. The course encourages students to develop both through sustained work, discussion and exposure to different research areas.'
]});
chapterStudyNotes[2].push({title:'9. Explaining results without overstating them',points:[
 'Technical communication should preserve the distinction between what the data show and what the researcher believes the data imply. A slide may present a measured trend, while the speaker explains a possible mechanism. Make the difference visible through wording such as “we observed” and “this may indicate.” If alternative explanations remain plausible, state them or identify what further work would distinguish them.',
 'A conclusion should answer the stated objective and be proportional to the design. Avoid broad statements that imply causation from a descriptive comparison or generalize beyond the sample and conditions. Include limitations when they affect interpretation, but do not bury the main result in a list of every imaginable caveat. The task is to communicate a balanced, evidence-based conclusion.',
 'Visual emphasis also affects interpretation. A large number, bold color or cropped axis can imply more certainty or magnitude than the underlying data warrant. Present uncertainty clearly and use comparable scales when comparing results. A technically accurate figure can still mislead if its design encourages the audience to draw an unsupported conclusion.'
]});
